{
  "status": "CORRECTED 2026-11-25. The first version of this file was wrong; see 'what_was_wrong' below. The correction came from the mac's review (dfd0242) and is verified here against the same committed grid runs.",
  "what_was_wrong": {
    "claim": "'preprocessing makes the scatter estimate over-confident by about 40 %', from a median dispersion ratio of 1.39 against 1.01 for raw.",
    "error": "the ratio was computed by POOLING f_beta = 0 and f_beta = 1 within each radius cell. Those two sub-populations have systematically OPPOSITE a offsets -- 8 of 8 cells flip sign, in both conditions -- so pooling adds between-group variance to the numerator while the denominator, the posterior width, is unchanged. The ratio measured a mixture, not a posterior.",
    "consequence": "the headline claim is withdrawn. The posterior intervals are correctly sized.",
    "also_withdrawn": "the proposed hull-jitter cause. It was an explanation for an effect that is not there."
  },
  "corrected_finding": {
    "within_f_beta_dispersion_ratio": {
      "raw_all": 0.8847753260916265,
      "raw_H1": 0.7894258775227596,
      "preprocessed_all": 0.9585917614587832,
      "preprocessed_H1": 1.0011123425689588,
      "reading": "~1 means the posterior width matches the real scatter. Preprocessed is 1.00 at the H1 radii, so it is NOT over-confident. Raw is 0.79, slightly conservative."
    },
    "pooled_ratio_for_contrast": {
      "raw_H1": 0.9937559466245278,
      "preprocessed_H1": 1.518914357723571
    },
    "real_effect": "coverage of a_true IS short in the preprocessed condition (0.708 at the H1 radii against a nominal 0.90), but the cause is a systematic f_beta-dependent OFFSET in the recovered radius, not an interval that is too narrow."
  },
  "mechanism": {
    "proposed_by": "the mac (WORKLOG 2026-11-25), with a toy model in scripts/toy_boundary_coverage.py",
    "account": "f_beta,true is 0 or 1, exactly on its prior boundary, so the posterior is truncated and f_hat is pushed inward. Because f_beta and a are correlated in the posterior, that inward push drags a_hat with it, in opposite directions at the two boundaries.",
    "evidence_from_this_grid": {
      "f_hat_pushed_inward_by_posterior_SD": {
        "raw_f0": 1.21,
        "raw_f1": -1.13,
        "preprocessed_f0": 1.14,
        "preprocessed_f1": -1.13
      },
      "median_posterior_corr_f_a_at_H1_radii": {
        "raw": -0.332,
        "preprocessed": -0.773
      },
      "toy_model_coverage_at_that_correlation": {
        "rho_-0.30": 0.89,
        "rho_-0.70": 0.835,
        "rho_-0.80": 0.787
      },
      "observed_coverage_at_H1_radii": {
        "raw": 0.9,
        "preprocessed": 0.708
      },
      "reading": "raw at rho = -0.33 should give about 0.89 and gives 0.900. Preprocessed at rho = -0.77 should give about 0.80 and gives 0.708 -- the right direction and roughly the right size, from a 2-D Gaussian toy. The stronger f-a correlation in the preprocessed window is why the same boundary effect costs it more coverage."
    }
  },
  "what_this_means_for_E1": "nothing in the verdict changes. W is 0.000000 in all 640 runs either way, and coverage was a descriptive output, not a criterion. What changes is the interpretation: this is an artefact of putting f_beta,true on its prior boundary, not a property of preprocessing.",
